      "text": "To determine the possible degrees of the remainder when a polynomial \\( P(x) \\) is divided by \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\), we need to leverage the Remainder Theorem, which states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial \\( P(x) \\) by a linear divisor \\( ax + b \\) is a constant. \n\nIn this case, the divisor is \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\), which is not a linear polynomial but a quadratic polynomial of degree 2. For any non-constant polynomial \\( P(x) \\) with a degree that is less than this quadratic divisor, the degree of the remainder \\( R(x) \\) will be less than the highest degree of the divisor. \n\nGiven that the degree of the divisor \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\) is 2, the maximum degree of the remainder \\( R(x) \\) must also be less than 2. Therefore, the possible degrees for the remainder are 0 and 1.\n\nThe final answer will be expressed in \\boxed{} notation. Let's write a Python code snippet to verify this reasoning:\n\n```python\n# The degree of the divisor 2x^2 - 7x + 18 is 2.\ndegree_divisor = 2\n\n# The possible degrees of the remainder are less than the degree of the divisor.\npossible_degrees = [0, 1]\n\nprint(possible_degrees)\n```",
